      "title": [
       {
        "type": "heading6",
        "text": "Manual import",
        "spans": [],
        "direction": "ltr"
       }
      ],
      "body_text": [
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "While the preferred method for integrating design system assets is via npm packages, there may be situations where importing npm packages isn't feasible. In such cases, you can manually copy the necessary files and code snippets into your codebase. This approach might be necessary if you're working on a legacy system with limited control over the technology stack, or simply want to experiment with the design system before making more significant changes to your workflow.",
        "spans": [],
        "direction": "ltr"
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "For scenarios like these, the @aalto-dx/react-components repository includes a straightforward static example of how to manually incorporate essential design system code into a web project. You can find the example files and detailed instructions in the static-starter folder located in the project root.",
        "spans": [],
        "direction": "ltr"
       },
       {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "However, it's important to emphasise that this is not the recommended method for using the design system. We strongly advise aiming for a modern development setup that includes version control, preprocessing, bundling, and other best practices to ensure maintainability, efficiency, and overall software quality.",
        "spans": [],
        "direction": "ltr"
       }
      ],
      "caption": [],
      "cta_label": [],
